==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|bespeken}}, {{m|enm|bispeken}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|*bespecan}}, {{m|ang|besprecan||to speak about, speak against, accuse of, claim at law, complain}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*bisprekaną||to discuss, blame}}, equivalent to {{prefix|be|speak|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{m|sco|bespeke||to beseech, speak or negotiate with}}, {{etyl|fy|-}} {{m|fy|besprekke||to discuss}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|bespreken||to discuss, review, debate}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|besprechen||to discuss, review, talk about}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA| [bɪˈspiːk]|lang= en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|bespeaks|bespeaking|bespoke|past2=bespake|past2_qual=archaic|bespoken|past_ptc2=bespoke|past_ptc2_qual=archaic}}

#{{lb|en|transitive}} To [[speak]] about; tell of; [[relate]]; [[discuss]].
#*'''2006''', Janet Jaymes, ''Dirty Laundry: A Memoir'':
#*:But to '''bespeak''' of a love, heavily weighed upon a heart, toward someone opposing those sentiments encourages foolish and embarrassing repercussions he will never know about.
#{{lb|en|transitive}} To speak for beforehand; [[engage]] in advance; make arrangements for; [[order]] or [[reserve]] [[in advance]].
#:{{ux|en|to '''bespeak''' a seat in a theatre}}
#*Sir {{w|Walter Scott}} (1771-1832)
#*:concluding, naturally, that to gratify his avarice was to '''bespeak''' his favour
#{{lb|en|transitive}} To [[stipulate]], [[solicit]], [[ask for]], or [[request]], as in a favour.
#:{{ux|en|to '''bespeak''' a calm hearing;&emsp; I '''bespeak''' your patience in advance.}}
#{{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[forbode]]; [[foretell]].
#{{lb|en|transitive|archaic|poetic}} To speak to; [[address]].
#*{{w|John Dryden}} (1631-1700)
#*:He thus the queen '''bespoke'''.
#{{lb|en|transitive}} To [[betoken]]; [[show]]; [[indicate]]; [[foretell]]; [[suggest]].
#:{{ux|en|This act '''bespeaks''' his kindness.}}
#*{{w|Jonathan Swift}} (1667–1745)
#*:[They] '''bespoke''' dangers{{...}}in order to scare the allies.
#*{{w|John Locke}} (1632-1705)
#*:When the abbot of St. Martin was born, he had so little the figure of a man that it '''bespoke''' him rather a monster.
#*{{RQ:EHough PrqsPrc|I|0108}}
#*:This new-comer was a man who in any company would have seemed striking.{{...}}He was smooth-faced, and his fresh skin and well-developed figure '''bespoke''' the man in good physical condition through active exercise, yet well content with the world's apportionment.
#{{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[speak up]] or [[speak out|out]]; [[exclaim]]; speak.

